<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleanor of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1241">1241</a>–<a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as the first wife of Edward I, whom she married in 1254. A daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a>, she inherited the county of Ponthieu from her mother in 1279. After her death in 1290 Edward raised the Eleanor crosses to mark the stages of her funeral procession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-castile.jpg" alt="Eleanor of Castile" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: History England Westminster abbey Souvenir book · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleonora_by_Torel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eleanor was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, the second of five children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> and Joan of Ponthieu, probably towards the end of 1241. The courts of her father and of her half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> were known for their literary atmosphere, and both kings encouraged a broad education of the royal children. In 1252 Alfonso revived an ancestral claim to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> countered with diplomatic and military moves. The two kings agreed that Eleanor would marry Henry's son Edward, who held the title of Duke of Gascony, and that Alfonso would transfer his Gascon claims to him. They were married at the monastery of Las Huelgas, Burgos, on 1 November 1254, when she was 13.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the Second Barons' War Eleanor held Windsor Castle and baronial prisoners for Edward. After the royalist defeat at the Battle of Lewes in 1264 Simon de Montfort, suspecting that she was seeking troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, ordered her removed from Windsor, and she was confined at Westminster Palace. In 1270 she and Edward left to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. They reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in May 1271, where she gave birth to a daughter, Joan of Acre. In June 1272 an assassin wounded Edward in the arm with a dagger thought to have been poisoned.</p><p>As queen Eleanor acquired estates worth about £2,600 a year between 1274 and 1290, an amount no English queen had held before, among them Leeds Castle. Some were bought from landholders who had fallen into arrears with Jewish moneylenders, which made her disliked and brought her into conflict with the church. She maintained the only royal scriptorium known in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> at the time and was a patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a>. She bore between 14 and 17 children, of whom six survived to adulthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late 1287, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, Eleanor was recorded as suffering from a double quartan fever, which suggests a strain of malaria. She died at Harby near Lincoln on 28 November 1290. Her embalmed body was carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, and Edward ordered a memorial cross at each overnight stop between Lincoln and Westminster. These monuments, modelled on crosses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that marked the funeral procession of Louis IX, stood at twelve places, ending at Charing. Only three survive, none complete, and the cross at Geddington is the best preserved.</p><p>Her later reputation was shaped by conflicting accounts. In 1586 the antiquarian William Camden first published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the tale that she saved Edward's life at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> by sucking the poison from his wound, and he attributed the crosses to Edward's grief for a heroic wife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleanor of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1279 the county passed through <b>Eleanor of Castile</b> to her husband Edward I of England.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ponthieu">County of Ponthieu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Crown of Castile. Died in Harby, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.23,-0.68&amp;year=1272" aria-label="Harby on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Eleanor of Castile"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4965 5578h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4965 5578h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.23,-0.68&amp;year=1272">What was here in 1272? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
